+     it is, while the evaluation which synthesizes the loop body creates it
+     with fresh ids: joining the exit context with the context at such an exit
+     then introduces a region abstraction which is missing from the exit
+     context, and the two do not match. In that case, we evaluate the body a
+     second time, which refreshes the ids of everything the iteration creates,
+     and join the contexts of both evaluations: this introduces the necessary
+     abstractions (upstream Aeneas 86158eb, applied to the exits of this fork).
+
+     Loans created by the iteration which are held by anonymous values or
+     region abstractions do not need this (the joins move them to region
+     abstractions, which the synthesis matches up to their ids), and the join
+     over two evaluations is not always possible for them (e.g., the shared
+     loans of a static global read in the iteration: "Markers remain after
+     ownership reduction"), so we only refresh the exits which need it.
+
